README-VI -- two vi editors, and which one to takeTake `elvis' unless you have a reason not to: its whole manual and sourceare on this disk, and it brings a read-only `view' and a few helpers.Take `vi' for the small plain one that asks for nothing but a termcap, orif you cannot give an editor a /dd/tmp.THE FILES---------  file              bytes  cio  module    source      docs  ----------------------------------------------------------------  vi                81468  no   vi        SRC/pvic    DOC/vi  REBUILT/vi_1.0    78496  no   vi_1.0    SRC/pvic    DOC/vi  elvis            111944  no   elvis     SRC/elvis   DOC/elvis  view              15132  no   view      SRC/elvis   DOC/elvis  REBUILT/vi.elvis  15140  no   vi.elvis  SRC/elvis   DOC/elvis  PVIC       vi, REBUILT/vi_1.0             two editions of one program  Elvis 1.7  elvis, view, REBUILT/vi.elvis  one program, three namesNeither needs a Microware module.  Each file has a module name of its own;if you add a vi of yours beside them, give its module its own name too,because once a module is resident OS-9 finds it by that name, whateverpath you type.WHAT EACH NEEDS BESIDES ITSELF------------------------------  vi, vi_1.0       /dd/sys/termcap  elvis            /dd/tmp, /h0/sys/termcap, /dd/SYS/password  view, vi.elvis   CMDS/elvis, which they runelvis stops before drawing anything if /dd/tmp is missing:    Can't create temp file... Does directory "/dd/tmp" exist?This disk has one; the system you copy elvis to may not.  Make it,publicly writable (`makdir /dd/tmp'), or name another directory beforeelvis opens anything:    setenv EXINIT "set directory=/dd/USR"The same line works in an .exrc.Both editors read the termcap entry from the TERMCAP environment variablefirst, so /h0 is not needed -- SYS/login sets it.  On a terminal neitherknows, run `. /dd/SYS/termcap.entry' first.Both edit OS-9's CR-terminated text as it is.To leave either: ESC, then `:q!' and Enter to quit without saving, or`:wq' and Enter to save and quit.vi -- the small plain one-------------------------  PVIC 1.0a, the Portable VI Clone, public domain.  The core vi command  set and the common options (autoindent, ignorecase, showmatch, tabstop),  without wrapmargin, shiftwidth, paragraphs or sections.  It has ex mode.  REBUILT/vi_1.0 is PVIC 1.0, the edition before.  DOC/vi has the options, the environment it reads and the termcap entries  it uses; `:help' gives the command summary.  For the commands themselves  any vi guide will do.elvis -- the one with everything around it------------------------------------------  Elvis 1.7, built from CMDS/archives/elvis1.7.lzh: a close clone of vi,  not an exact one.  It has more `:set' options than `vi', and is the only  one here with colour and attribute handling (`charattr'), digraphs and a  configurable `equalprg'.  DOC/elvis holds its full manual set.  It comes with:      view              elvis opened read-only      ref               look a C function up from a tags file      fmt               format text      elvprsv, elvrec   preserve and recover a session across a crash      REBUILT/vi.elvis, elvis_input, ctags.elvis  `view' and `vi.elvis' run `elvis', so copy it with them.CHOOSING--------  You want a manual to read when something misbehaves    elvis  You want the plainest thing that edits                 vi  You cannot give an editor a /dd/tmp                    vi  You want read-only browsing                            view (needs elvis)